1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,760
Why does a 3 p.m.

2
00:00:01,760 --> 00:00:03,840
coffee still cost you sleep at midnight,

3
00:00:03,840 --> 00:00:05,680
when you feel completely fine?

4
00:00:05,680 --> 00:00:07,120
You drank it hours ago.

5
00:00:07,120 --> 00:00:08,880
The mug is already washed and back

6
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:09,760
in the cupboard.

7
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:10,960
You don't feel wired,

8
00:00:10,960 --> 00:00:12,560
you climb into bed on time,

9
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:14,080
and you drift off more or less

10
00:00:14,080 --> 00:00:14,800
as usual.

11
00:00:14,800 --> 00:00:15,520
In the morning,

12
00:00:15,520 --> 00:00:16,960
you'd swear you slept fine.

13
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:19,040
But a sleep monitor might disagree.

14
00:00:19,040 --> 00:00:20,480
Here's the thing most people get

15
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:21,360
backwards:

16
00:00:21,360 --> 00:00:23,600
caffeine doesn't actually give you energy.

17
00:00:23,600 --> 00:00:25,360
It blocks the message that says you're

18
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:25,920
tired.

19
00:00:25,920 --> 00:00:28,000
And that message doesn't go away just

20
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:29,440
because you stopped hearing it.

21
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:31,440
In this video we'll meet the messenger,

22
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:33,040
the bean that blocks it,

23
00:00:33,040 --> 00:00:34,800
and the clock that decides how long

24
00:00:34,800 --> 00:00:35,848
it stays blocked.

25
00:00:35,848 --> 00:00:37,688
So let's follow the message,

26
00:00:37,688 --> 00:00:39,368
from the moment it's written to the

27
00:00:39,368 --> 00:00:41,368
moment it finally gets delivered.

28
00:00:41,368 --> 00:00:42,888
For a 3 p.m.

29
00:00:42,888 --> 00:00:43,528
coffee,

30
00:00:43,528 --> 00:00:44,968
that can be well after you've gone

31
00:00:44,968 --> 00:00:45,608
to bed.

32
00:00:45,608 --> 00:00:46,968
Every hour you're awake,

33
00:00:46,968 --> 00:00:49,448
your brain cells burn through energy,

34
00:00:49,448 --> 00:00:50,968
and one of the leftovers is a

35
00:00:50,968 --> 00:00:52,808
molecule called adenosine.

36
00:00:52,808 --> 00:00:54,968
Think of adenosine as a mail carrier

37
00:00:54,968 --> 00:00:56,568
with one letter to deliver,

38
00:00:56,568 --> 00:00:57,608
over and over.

39
00:00:57,608 --> 00:00:58,968
The letter says one word:

40
00:00:58,968 --> 00:00:59,768
tired.

41
00:00:59,768 --> 00:01:00,888
No return address,

42
00:01:00,888 --> 00:01:01,848
no small talk.

43
00:01:01,848 --> 00:01:04,008
Your brain has mailboxes built just for

44
00:01:04,008 --> 00:01:04,968
those letters,

45
00:01:04,968 --> 00:01:07,048
called adenosine receptors.

46
00:01:07,048 --> 00:01:08,888
Every time a letter drops in,

47
00:01:08,888 --> 00:01:10,328
the parts of your brain that keep

48
00:01:10,328 --> 00:01:11,928
you alert slow down a little.

49
00:01:11,928 --> 00:01:13,128
The longer you're awake,

50
00:01:13,128 --> 00:01:14,380
the more letters arrive.

51
00:01:14,380 --> 00:01:15,820
By the end of a long day,

52
00:01:15,820 --> 00:01:17,740
the mail carrier is shoving them through

53
00:01:17,740 --> 00:01:18,780
with both hands.

54
00:01:18,780 --> 00:01:19,420
That heavy,

55
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:21,660
yawning feeling is what scientists call

56
00:01:21,660 --> 00:01:22,540
sleep pressure.

57
00:01:22,540 --> 00:01:25,420
Adenosine isn't the only thing behind it,

58
00:01:25,420 --> 00:01:27,020
but scientists think it's one of the

59
00:01:27,020 --> 00:01:27,740
big ones.

60
00:01:27,740 --> 00:01:28,620
Then you sleep,

61
00:01:28,620 --> 00:01:30,700
the letters get cleared away overnight,

62
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:32,860
and you wake up with empty mailboxes.

63
00:01:32,860 --> 00:01:33,580
Sleep is,

64
00:01:33,580 --> 00:01:34,620
among other things,

65
00:01:34,620 --> 00:01:36,300
your brain doing its paperwork.

66
00:01:36,300 --> 00:01:37,820
It's a tidy system,

67
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:39,580
right up until someone tampers with the

68
00:01:39,580 --> 00:01:40,060
mail.

69
00:01:40,060 --> 00:01:41,260
Enter caffeine.

70
00:01:41,260 --> 00:01:43,100
About fifteen to thirty minutes after your

71
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:43,980
first sip,

72
00:01:43,980 --> 00:01:45,180
it starts to kick in.

73
00:01:45,180 --> 00:01:47,100
Caffeine happens to be shaped a lot

74
00:01:47,100 --> 00:01:48,380
like adenosine.

75
00:01:48,380 --> 00:01:50,220
Close enough to slide right into the

76
00:01:50,220 --> 00:01:51,260
mailbox slot.

77
00:01:51,260 --> 00:01:52,300
But it isn't a letter,

78
00:01:52,300 --> 00:01:53,938
so it doesn't deliver anything.

79
00:01:53,938 --> 00:01:55,138
It just sits there,

80
00:01:55,138 --> 00:01:56,978
wearing a sign that says closed.

81
00:01:56,978 --> 00:01:59,538
Now the tiredness letters arrive and find

82
00:01:59,538 --> 00:02:00,578
the slot taken.

83
00:02:00,578 --> 00:02:01,698
They can't get in,

84
00:02:01,698 --> 00:02:03,458
so your brain never gets the message

85
00:02:03,458 --> 00:02:04,418
that you're tired.

86
00:02:04,418 --> 00:02:05,858
It checks the mailbox,

87
00:02:05,858 --> 00:02:07,138
finds nothing new,

88
00:02:07,138 --> 00:02:08,818
and decides you must be fine.

89
00:02:08,818 --> 00:02:10,018
That's the whole trick.

90
00:02:10,018 --> 00:02:11,858
Caffeine doesn't hand you fuel;

91
00:02:11,858 --> 00:02:13,618
a cup of black coffee has about

92
00:02:13,618 --> 00:02:14,658
two calories.

93
00:02:14,658 --> 00:02:16,258
It just stops your brain from reading

94
00:02:16,258 --> 00:02:17,218
the bad news.

95
00:02:17,218 --> 00:02:18,658
At everyday doses,

96
00:02:18,658 --> 00:02:20,738
blocking adenosine is the main thing

97
00:02:20,738 --> 00:02:22,418
caffeine does in your brain.

98
00:02:22,418 --> 00:02:23,058
Meanwhile,

99
00:02:23,058 --> 00:02:24,018
you're still awake,

100
00:02:24,018 --> 00:02:25,938
so adenosine keeps being made.

101
00:02:25,938 --> 00:02:27,378
The mail carrier knocks,

102
00:02:27,378 --> 00:02:28,018
waits,

103
00:02:28,018 --> 00:02:29,858
and leaves another letter on the step.

104
00:02:29,858 --> 00:02:31,298
The letters don't vanish;

105
00:02:31,298 --> 00:02:32,836
they stack up outside the door.

106
00:02:32,836 --> 00:02:35,236
Caffeine doesn't cancel your tiredness,

107
00:02:35,236 --> 00:02:36,436
it puts it on hold.

108
00:02:36,436 --> 00:02:38,596
And everything on hold gets delivered

109
00:02:38,596 --> 00:02:39,316
eventually.

110
00:02:39,316 --> 00:02:40,996
So how long does caffeine stay in

111
00:02:40,996 --> 00:02:41,796
the slot?

112
00:02:41,796 --> 00:02:42,996
That's up to your liver,

113
00:02:42,996 --> 00:02:44,836
which slowly breaks caffeine down and

114
00:02:44,836 --> 00:02:45,796
clears it out.

115
00:02:45,796 --> 00:02:48,356
Scientists measure this with a half-life:

116
00:02:48,356 --> 00:02:49,796
the time it takes your body to

117
00:02:49,796 --> 00:02:50,836
get rid of half of it.

118
00:02:50,836 --> 00:02:52,596
For a typical healthy adult,

119
00:02:52,596 --> 00:02:54,836
caffeine's half-life is around four to

120
00:02:54,836 --> 00:02:56,196
five hours on average.

121
00:02:56,196 --> 00:02:57,956
That's a long time for something you

122
00:02:57,956 --> 00:02:59,316
finished in ten minutes.

123
00:02:59,316 --> 00:03:00,916
And that average hides a lot of

124
00:03:00,916 --> 00:03:01,636
wobble.

125
00:03:01,636 --> 00:03:03,156
Some people clear half of it in

126
00:03:03,156 --> 00:03:04,116
about two hours.

127
00:03:04,116 --> 00:03:04,836
For others,

128
00:03:04,836 --> 00:03:06,356
it takes eight hours or more.

129
00:03:06,356 --> 00:03:08,116
Your speed depends on things like your

130
00:03:08,116 --> 00:03:08,756
genes,

131
00:03:08,756 --> 00:03:09,556
your age,

132
00:03:09,556 --> 00:03:10,596
whether you smoke,

133
00:03:10,596 --> 00:03:11,751
and even your diet.

134
00:03:11,751 --> 00:03:14,231
Pregnancy and some medicines can slow it

135
00:03:14,231 --> 00:03:14,951
down too.

136
00:03:14,951 --> 00:03:15,751
If that's you,

137
00:03:15,751 --> 00:03:17,751
the person to ask is your doctor,

138
00:03:17,751 --> 00:03:19,111
not a cartoon bean.

139
00:03:19,111 --> 00:03:19,751
Either way,

140
00:03:19,751 --> 00:03:21,591
caffeine never leaves all at once.

141
00:03:21,591 --> 00:03:22,791
After one half-life,

142
00:03:22,791 --> 00:03:23,991
half of it is gone.

143
00:03:23,991 --> 00:03:24,871
After another,

144
00:03:24,871 --> 00:03:25,991
half of what's left.

145
00:03:25,991 --> 00:03:27,511
It leaves the way a guest leaves

146
00:03:27,511 --> 00:03:28,391
a party:

147
00:03:28,391 --> 00:03:29,111
slowly,

148
00:03:29,111 --> 00:03:31,111
and saying goodbye several times.

149
00:03:31,111 --> 00:03:32,631
Let's run the numbers on that 3

150
00:03:32,631 --> 00:03:33,351
p.m.

151
00:03:33,351 --> 00:03:34,151
coffee.

152
00:03:34,151 --> 00:03:35,724
Say it's a small cup with about

153
00:03:35,724 --> 00:03:37,511
a hundred milligrams of caffeine.

154
00:03:37,511 --> 00:03:38,711
Cups vary a lot,

155
00:03:38,711 --> 00:03:40,471
so this is just a round number.

156
00:03:40,471 --> 00:03:42,631
And say your half-life is five hours,

157
00:03:42,631 --> 00:03:44,071
near the middle of the range.

158
00:03:44,071 --> 00:03:45,591
Here's what's still in you as the

159
00:03:45,591 --> 00:03:46,631
evening goes on.

160
00:03:46,631 --> 00:03:48,071
By 8 p.m.,

161
00:03:48,071 --> 00:03:50,216
about fifty milligrams are still on duty.

162
00:03:50,216 --> 00:03:51,176
By midnight,

163
00:03:51,176 --> 00:03:52,696
nine hours after the cup,

164
00:03:52,696 --> 00:03:54,456
you've still got roughly thirty.

165
00:03:54,456 --> 00:03:55,576
That's more than a quarter of the

166
00:03:55,576 --> 00:03:56,136
cup,

167
00:03:56,136 --> 00:03:58,056
sitting in your mailboxes while you try

168
00:03:58,056 --> 00:03:58,696
to sleep.

169
00:03:58,696 --> 00:04:00,456
If your half-life is closer to eight

170
00:04:00,456 --> 00:04:01,016
hours,

171
00:04:01,016 --> 00:04:02,296
it's nearer to half.

172
00:04:02,296 --> 00:04:03,816
And if you had a big coffee-shop

173
00:04:03,816 --> 00:04:04,296
cup,

174
00:04:04,296 --> 00:04:05,656
start with a bigger number.

175
00:04:05,656 --> 00:04:07,656
You probably won't feel thirty milligrams

176
00:04:07,656 --> 00:04:08,456
as a buzz.

177
00:04:08,456 --> 00:04:09,896
But it doesn't need to buzz to

178
00:04:09,896 --> 00:04:11,336
keep a few slots closed.

179
00:04:11,336 --> 00:04:13,336
The closed signs are smaller now,

180
00:04:13,336 --> 00:04:14,936
but they're still up at bedtime.

181
00:04:14,936 --> 00:04:16,376
And at 7 a.m.,

182
00:04:16,376 --> 00:04:17,816
sixteen hours later,

183
00:04:17,816 --> 00:04:20,136
there's still about ten milligrams left.

184
00:04:20,136 --> 00:04:21,896
Just in time for the next coffee.

185
00:04:21,896 --> 00:04:24,056
So does that leftover caffeine actually

186
00:04:24,056 --> 00:04:25,256
cost you sleep?

187
00:04:25,256 --> 00:04:26,856
In 2013,

188
00:04:26,856 --> 00:04:29,096
researchers led by Christopher Drake

189
00:04:29,096 --> 00:04:30,357
tested exactly that.

190
00:04:30,357 --> 00:04:32,437
Twelve healthy sleepers took a caffeine

191
00:04:32,437 --> 00:04:33,397
pill at bedtime,

192
00:04:33,397 --> 00:04:35,237
three hours before bed,

193
00:04:35,237 --> 00:04:36,917
or six hours before bed.

194
00:04:36,917 --> 00:04:38,757
Each of them tried every version,

195
00:04:38,757 --> 00:04:40,117
plus a dummy pill,

196
00:04:40,117 --> 00:04:42,117
and nobody knew which night was which.

197
00:04:42,117 --> 00:04:43,557
They went to bed at their usual

198
00:04:43,557 --> 00:04:44,117
time,

199
00:04:44,117 --> 00:04:44,837
at home,

200
00:04:44,837 --> 00:04:46,677
wearing a headband that recorded their

201
00:04:46,677 --> 00:04:47,637
brain waves.

202
00:04:47,637 --> 00:04:48,837
The dose was big:

203
00:04:48,837 --> 00:04:50,357
400 milligrams,

204
00:04:50,357 --> 00:04:52,357
roughly four small cups of coffee.

205
00:04:52,357 --> 00:04:54,277
That's the most the FDA considers

206
00:04:54,277 --> 00:04:56,117
generally fine for a whole day,

207
00:04:56,117 --> 00:04:57,317
in a single pill.

208
00:04:57,317 --> 00:04:59,077
Even when the pill came six hours

209
00:04:59,077 --> 00:04:59,957
before bed,

210
00:04:59,957 --> 00:05:01,397
they lost more than an hour of

211
00:05:01,397 --> 00:05:01,957
sleep.

212
00:05:01,957 --> 00:05:03,557
And here's the strange part.

213
00:05:03,557 --> 00:05:05,237
In their own sleep diaries,

214
00:05:05,237 --> 00:05:07,237
the six-hour nights didn't clearly stand

215
00:05:07,237 --> 00:05:07,637
out.

216
00:05:07,637 --> 00:05:08,917
The headband noticed;

217
00:05:08,917 --> 00:05:10,469
the sleepers mostly didn't.

218
00:05:10,469 --> 00:05:11,989
It was a small study with a

219
00:05:11,989 --> 00:05:12,949
large dose,

220
00:05:12,949 --> 00:05:14,549
so it's not the final word.

221
00:05:14,549 --> 00:05:16,389
But it showed caffeine can take sleep

222
00:05:16,389 --> 00:05:17,109
quietly,

223
00:05:17,109 --> 00:05:18,629
without you feeling robbed.

224
00:05:18,629 --> 00:05:20,069
Which is the most polite kind of

225
00:05:20,069 --> 00:05:20,709
theft.

226
00:05:20,709 --> 00:05:22,389
One study is one study,

227
00:05:22,389 --> 00:05:23,909
so here's the bigger picture.

228
00:05:23,909 --> 00:05:25,589
In 2023,

229
00:05:25,589 --> 00:05:27,989
a team pulled together 24 studies on

230
00:05:27,989 --> 00:05:29,429
caffeine and sleep.

231
00:05:29,429 --> 00:05:30,229
On average,

232
00:05:30,229 --> 00:05:32,709
caffeine cut total sleep by about 45

233
00:05:32,709 --> 00:05:33,349
minutes.

234
00:05:33,349 --> 00:05:35,349
It added roughly nine minutes to falling

235
00:05:35,349 --> 00:05:36,069
asleep,

236
00:05:36,069 --> 00:05:37,829
and about twelve more minutes awake in

237
00:05:37,829 --> 00:05:38,389
the night.

238
00:05:38,389 --> 00:05:40,149
It also changed the kind of sleep

239
00:05:40,149 --> 00:05:40,709
you get.

240
00:05:40,709 --> 00:05:42,469
There was a bit more light sleep,

241
00:05:42,469 --> 00:05:44,709
and about eleven minutes less deep sleep,

242
00:05:44,709 --> 00:05:45,349
the slow,

243
00:05:45,349 --> 00:05:47,347
heavy kind that's hardest to wake from.

244
00:05:47,347 --> 00:05:48,547
From their numbers,

245
00:05:48,547 --> 00:05:50,787
the authors suggested stopping coffee

246
00:05:50,787 --> 00:05:53,347
about nine hours before bed to avoid

247
00:05:53,347 --> 00:05:54,547
losing sleep time.

248
00:05:54,547 --> 00:05:55,987
For a 3 p.m.

249
00:05:55,987 --> 00:05:56,627
cup,

250
00:05:56,627 --> 00:05:58,307
that means bed no earlier than about

251
00:05:58,307 --> 00:05:58,947
midnight.

252
00:05:58,947 --> 00:06:00,547
For a pre-workout powder,

253
00:06:00,547 --> 00:06:02,307
with about twice the caffeine,

254
00:06:02,307 --> 00:06:04,467
they suggested more than thirteen hours.

255
00:06:04,467 --> 00:06:06,387
So a 6 p.m.

256
00:06:06,387 --> 00:06:08,467
gym session would mean a bedtime around

257
00:06:08,467 --> 00:06:09,667
7 the next morning.

258
00:06:09,667 --> 00:06:10,627
It's an average,

259
00:06:10,627 --> 00:06:11,587
not a law,

260
00:06:11,587 --> 00:06:13,427
and some people are far more sensitive

261
00:06:13,427 --> 00:06:14,067
than others.

262
00:06:14,067 --> 00:06:16,467
But it explains why your afternoon coffee

263
00:06:16,467 --> 00:06:18,307
keeps showing up for the night shift.

264
00:06:18,307 --> 00:06:18,547
So,

265
00:06:18,547 --> 00:06:20,387
how does caffeine steal your sleep?

266
00:06:20,387 --> 00:06:21,987
It never gave you energy in the

267
00:06:21,987 --> 00:06:22,664
first place.

268
00:06:22,664 --> 00:06:24,744
It sat in your brain's mailboxes,

269
00:06:24,744 --> 00:06:26,264
wearing a closed sign,

270
00:06:26,264 --> 00:06:28,184
while the tiredness letters piled up

271
00:06:28,184 --> 00:06:28,824
outside.

272
00:06:28,824 --> 00:06:30,424
Your liver cleared it slowly,

273
00:06:30,424 --> 00:06:31,304
half at a time,

274
00:06:31,304 --> 00:06:32,424
over many hours.

275
00:06:32,424 --> 00:06:34,344
And for a 3 p.m.

276
00:06:34,344 --> 00:06:35,064
coffee,

277
00:06:35,064 --> 00:06:36,664
enough can still be there at midnight

278
00:06:36,664 --> 00:06:38,264
to keep a few slots shut.

279
00:06:38,264 --> 00:06:39,464
Not enough to feel.

280
00:06:39,464 --> 00:06:41,144
Just enough to cost you sleep you

281
00:06:41,144 --> 00:06:42,584
might never notice losing.

282
00:06:42,584 --> 00:06:44,184
Like the sleepers in that study,

283
00:06:44,184 --> 00:06:45,704
you'd wake up and swear you slept

284
00:06:45,704 --> 00:06:46,264
fine.

285
00:06:46,264 --> 00:06:48,104
Tiredness doesn't get cancelled;

286
00:06:48,104 --> 00:06:49,544
it gets rescheduled.

287
00:06:49,544 --> 00:06:51,384
None of this makes coffee a villain.

288
00:06:51,384 --> 00:06:53,144
It does exactly what it's built to

289
00:06:53,144 --> 00:06:53,704
do,

290
00:06:53,704 --> 00:06:55,304
just for much longer than the cup

291
00:06:55,304 --> 00:06:55,944
suggests.

